welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is Professor Steven Pincus a  professor of history at Yale professor  Pincus teaches early modern British and  European history he is the author of  Protestantism and patriotism ideologies  and the making of English foreign policy  1652 1668 and England's Glorious  Revolution 1688 to 89 he has also  published numerous essays on the  cultural political and intellectual  history of early modern Britain today  we'll be talking with Professor Pincus  about his newest book called 1688 the  first modern revolution  welcome professor Pincus so pleased to  be here early in your newest book which  will be out in September you retell in a  significantly new way the story of the  Glorious Revolution  what is the Glorious Revolution so the  Glorious Revolution is an event and  which took place normally in 16 normally  considered to have happened in 1688 and  1689 in England in which the old King  James ii who happened to have been a  catholic was replaced by a new King  William and his wife who became Queen  Mary the second and Queen Mary actually  happened to be the daughter of James the  second and it's after William and Mary  that for example the College of William  and Mary in Virginia is named okay and  what drew you to this topic the story of  England's Glorious Revolution which for  a long time had had come to be seen as  marking England as as perhaps the first  modern nation of the first modern  parliamentary nation was always told as  an English story something in which they  were English causes and narrowly English  consequences and my previous work  convinced me of two things one was that  England very much was part of Europe in  the 17th century as much as as part of  Europe in the 21st century and that  trying to understand developments within  England in a narrowly English context  missed a good part of the story the  second thing that my earlier work  convinced me of was that in the 17th  century merchants and economics were an  increasingly important part of English  political and social life and older  accounts of the Glorious Revolution said  nothing about merchants and nothing  about economics okay so how does your  version of the revolution differ from  what's in the books now the normal story  the ubiquitous story of the Glorious  Revolution is that England's revolution  in 1688 89 was a conservative revolution  a sensible revolution and Unruh volution  Airy revolution something which bears no  resemblance to major revolutions like  the French Revolution the American  Revolution the Chinese Revolution the  Russian Revolution and my argument is  that that's exactly wrong that England's  England's revolution in 1688 89 was in  fact the first modern revolution it was  very much a radically transformative  revolution with everything that came  along with it it was a violent  revolution it was a socially divisive  revolution and it was a revolution which  involved large huge segments of the  overall English population  okay and how did you do your research  well the last major work on England's  Glorious Revolution based on on thorough  archival research was done by thomas  babington macaulay in the second quarter  of the 19th century and it stood to  reason that there was a lot of material  that became available since then and it  turned out they were there was a huge  amount of material so I went visited a  range of European archives I traveled to  local record offices local depositories  of of manuscripts  that had not existed in the 19th century  I visited collections of business  materials which weren't fully available  in the 19th century and I also used the  wealth of materials that had in various  ways made their ways into North American  archives both in Canada and in the u.s.  again which British historians have  traditionally ignored I say so I imagine  that's one of the reasons why the old  narrative has been so widely accepted  for so long are there any other reasons  well the the old narrative was accepted  for a long time because there were a  number of people invested in telling a  story that England was not like the rest  of Europe or like the rest of the world  um so the the key moments of the telling  of the story of England's Revolution of  1688 89 were in 1848 18-49 the period in  which the Europe was convulsed with  revolutions so thomas babington macaulay  was invested in saying that england  wasn't like like these other places on  the continent where there could be a  radical and destructive revolution that  england had what he said was a saving  revolution which prevented it from  having in the 17th century which  prevented it from having a violent  revolution in the 19th century an  earlier moment in which the story was  told was by Edmund Burke when he was  during the revolution the 1789  revolution in France in which he again  said England had a sensible and Glorious  Revolution  whereas France had a violent and  destructive revolution and the final  important phase of the telling of the  story was the 1930s when George McAuley  Trevelyan wanted to distinguish English  political culture from the from the  political culture that was leading to  fascism in Spain and in Italy and to  Nazism in Germany so in all three key  moments the English were invested in  telling a story about themselves which  was to suggest how different they were  from continental Europeans and  in doing your research were there any  any elements that surprised you yeah I  mean I think the biggest surprise was to  discover how important India and the  Empire more broadly was in the run-up  and the causes and in the consequences  of the Revolution so none of the books  about the none of the myriad books about  the the Glorious Revolution say anything  about the fact that James ii declared  war on the Mughal Empire in India in  1687 and that war proved to be a  disastrous failure and how excited and  interested most English people were  about India and the Empire more broadly  and how in fact the revolution of 1688  eighty-nine completely transformed  England's relationship with India and  also with the West Indies and and and  and North America so how was it changed  how was it work so james ii was a very  modern and forward-looking monarch who  thought that england's future as a major  european player could be secured by  creating a massive authoritarian  territorial empire overseas both in the  east indies and what's now India and  Indonesia  and in the West Indies meeting both the  Caribbean and North America so he tried  to consolidate centralize control and  used the revenues from this empire to  finance a huge standing army in England  now England had traditionally been a  place where there was no regular  standing army and James created one of  the most professional and efficient  armies the Europe had seen it had  between forty and fifty thousand men  whereas England traditionally had had an  army of maybe two thousand men so it was  this very sort of strong authoritarian  army which which he which he created  based on this authoritarian Empire  overseas and what happened at the  revolution was to transform the English  Empire the British Empire from being an  authoritarian torrid territorial Empire  into being a commercial empire when  where the emphasis was exclusively on  trade and to  form England itself from an agrarian  society into a manufacturing society  so without the Glorious Revolution in my  view there would have been a very  different British Empire in the 18th and  19th century and without the Glorious  Revolution in the late 17th century  there would have been no Industrial  Revolution in the late 18th and 19th  centuries thank you very much for being  here with us today and we'll look  forward to your book in September okay  it's been a pleasure for more  information about Professor Pincus and  his work please visit our website at  Yale ddu 